From: AlexProgrammerDE <40795980+AlexProgrammerDE@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 11:15:03 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] fix(input): restart completed Bedrock mining gestures

Require an active gesture when Java recognizes the same Bedrock mining target. Java 26.3 retains the previous target after completion, so a server-restored block accumulates progress without sending Start, stays inactive, and leaves cracks when attack is released.

Native Bedrock 1.26.60.5/protocol 2225 completes at 652, closes with Abort at 653 and starts the next attempt at 654, completing it at 676. Java's five-input pause advances only while hitting a block and can carry into later fresh presses. Replace it for survival continuation with a closing-frame pause advanced by game-mode ticks, including misses and released input. A count of two retains the closing frame across tick/callback order and admits continuation on the next frame. Keep the separate creative constant and Java sessions unchanged.

Credit the first damage update when continueDestroyBlock starts an automatic attempt. A fresh click already runs that update in the same tick; Java otherwise returns directly from an automatic start, making its attempt 23 input frames instead of the native 22. After the start, the target and held item are active, so the nested continuation takes the ordinary damage branch and does not restart again. The core separately owns packet completion, Abort and pending Start ordering.

Bounded read-only installed-client observations establish the inactive target, cooldown carry and retry-duration differences. REA retains the original native packet controls and the exact Java 26.3 runtime artifact inspection. Validate selectors, automatic progress, request framing and release behavior in the installed client; unit tests do not apply these mixins.
